\pard\plain \sa180\sb180\ltrch\loch {\f0\fs24\b0 Oh, here's the kicker. Being in a healthy relationship actually may feel alien, boring and "wrong". That's, again, because you don't have to angle or work for approval. It's just given to you, no strings attached. Paradoxical, that can be off putting because you don't feel the excitement of getting a fought over prize.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0 See, while the "spark" many people feel is a real thing, it's easy to confuse that for the familiar anxiety and fear of rejection you feel over someone giving inconsistent signals, which make you put on a performance of sorts, in order to win them over. That performance generally showing itself as fantasizing, idealizing, and so on.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0 As for the reality of having a healthy relationship born out of limerence: it does happen... but the chances are low. Mutual limerence is rare, and given how it's actually a coping mechanism, it requires a ton of self awareness to make things work out. It's not something to indulge in, there be dragons.}
\par\pard\plain \fi720\sl288\slmult1\ltrch\loch \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 The fantasizing is a form of self soothing. It makes you feel good, because it's rewarding. So, that lights up the reward systems in your brain. Those get trained to want more of the same reward, so those will prompt you to do it more. You can see how that's a cycle that spirals into obsessive thinking. In a way, it compares to addiction, except your addicted to thinking about the other person.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 So, when you actively try to "not think", your brain will scream, and push extra intrusive thoughts on you to make you chase the reward. The pains you feel are a comparable to withdrawal.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Getting rid of the habit consists of three parts.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 The first is, preferably, no contact. Especially if she's out of your life. Stop visiting her social media. Stop seeking her out online. Delete messages and pictures. Cut the cord.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 The second is learning to sit with the intrusive thoughts and feelings. The experience is highly unpleasant, but the idea is to notice them, label them, but choosing to not engage with them. Just let them drift by. Instead, you shift your focus on other areas. This is where mindfulness and grounding exercises are really helpful.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 The third is working on your own life. You'll need a replacement for the reward. Exercising is a rewarding activity. But basically anything that you feel is rewarding works. Wether that's traveling, helping someone else, cooking or whatever. The point is to do things that are rewarding, but also demonstrate to you that you are able to take care of yourself, give you confidence, and show you that you can build your own identity.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 None of those are quick fix solutions. That's where practicing honesty, compassion, self respect and kindness towards yourself are so very crucial. It takes time and patience, and recovery isn't linear. You'll have good and bad days.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 A big part of limerence is shame, guilt and regret. The secrecy and taboo over having this experience. It helps to talk to a therapist about this. But the thing is that a vast chunk of people go through this at any given moment. This includes yours truly as well. You're not alone.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 You don't control your feels either. You do control how you meet yourself.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 My reasons for quitting porn:}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 All that dopamine dumped into a short-term, unproductive activity.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 I want the love of a real woman, and crave the connection intimacy provides. When I see others going at it, this feeling rises, is quite powerful, and it sickens me that I watch others feeling this.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 The Neurobiological Blueprint}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 In reality it's much more complicated and intertwined, and this is an oversimplification to ease understanding. But conceptually, our brains evolved in the form of 3 hardware stacks over eons:}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 (1) The Lizard Brain (brainstem/basal ganglia), the most ancient/primal and base layer responsible for survival (fight or flight), when we perceive a threat, the Lizard (I usually visualize a T-rex) kicks on automatically and overrides all other brain function. When it never gets to rest, it gives you chronic anxiety;}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 (2) The Mouse Brain (limbic system), the mammalian layer responsible for seeking behavior, evolutionarily this evolved to drip dopamine which encourages us to exert effort in order to find food or shelter, think of a field mouse looking for food, it searches and stumbles but the promise of a reward/food keeps it going. When this is conditioned overtime on result-based/instant-dopamine, it makes you jaded;}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 (3) The Primate Brain (cerebral cortex), the most recent evolution responsible for executive function, seeking social connections, logic, this is the one used to write this article. When it is starved, it makes you lonely.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 To "hack" these conceptual hardware stacks in a sustainable and healthy way to cope with the modern world, you generally want to:}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 (1) Override the Lizard Brain, focus on the positive stuff and hold on to them for 15-20 seconds. This is the exact amount of time required to move an experience from short term memory into long term memory, structurally thickening the neural pathways for safety and joy. The Lizard Brain acts as the "Velcro for bad and Teflon for good" (Rick Hanson's Hardwiring Happiness), so consciously doing this over time is pivotal for overriding your default negativity bias.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 (2) Nurture the Mouse Brain, shift from cheap dopamine to effort-based dopamine. Anything that requires physical, mental, or creative exertion before getting the award, not one-click not pay2win, not variable slot machines. This teaches your brain to enjoy the reward along with the toil. Teaching the brain to attach dopamine to the effort rather than the outcome (instant-gratification) is the ultimate shield against anhedonia/apathy that accumulates overtime from relying on porn, doom-scrolling, p2w games, hyper-palatable processed foods, alcohol, one-click therapy, app-based socials and dating, etc., the list goes on. The general rule here is to never seek pleasure to subtract (numb/disassociate), but only to add.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 (3) Grow the Primate Brain, seek positive connection and help real people (not AI/chatbots). Humans are where we are today because the Primate Brain evolved to ensure resource sharing, offering potent biochemical rewards when we seek cooperation. When you help another person, you align your highest-functioning (but most easily overwhelmed) Primate Brain with the more primal Lizard and Mouse. This gives us both feelings of safety (belonging to a tribe) and a more potent biochemical cocktail (reward for effort) containing not just dopamine, but also oxytocin and vasopressin. These are chemically longer-lasting, more stable neuropeptides that physically calm the nervous system you can only get from interacting with another human being.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 I don't think any of this is really new, we've all heard it, read it, or been advised to do it at one point or another and I never took any of it to heart. But getting a cursory understanding of the underlying biology really helped me, or at least motivated me to take more action.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
